specs/band-flex-tails.md:9:- FLEX1, joystick side (-Y): carries the vibration motor and the five joystick switches SW1 to SW5, SW2 being the centre switch under the nub (17.11, 17.14). The ring has no record switch and no record button.
specs/band-flex-tails.md:35:| switch pad group count, FLEX1 | 5 | not a dimension | the five joystick switches SW1 to SW5 (SW2 centre) on the same tail; before v5: four switches plus a record switch |
specs/band-flex-tails.md:40:| centre switch | SW2 under the nub centre | not a dimension | one of the five switches (17.11, 17.15); there is no record switch and no record button |
specs/band-flex-tails.md:46:Length, width and path of each tail are open point 4: the architecture gives none. The 3D model (design/mech/thin/thin-notes.md, model v6.2) uses width 1.4 (X -3.3 to -1.9) and the floor slot 2.0 x 0.6 from X -3.5 to -1.5 (before v6.2 2.6 x 0.6 at X -3.5 to -0.9) at Y = +-5.9, with the bottom at Z -0.20 (US 7 and 8), -0.23, -0.63, -1.04, -1.45 and -1.85 (US 9 to 13) (17.26); a free volume 2.6 x 2.2 x 1.03 beside each slot under the board holds one fold (model v5 figure: X -3.5 to -0.9, Y +-3.4 to +-5.6, Z 0.40 to 1.43; not measured again for the shorter slot). Whether one fold of the 0.15 ribbon fits that height (bend radius) is the flex maker's (open point 4).
specs/cell.md:45:| running discharge | about 4.1 mA recording | not a dimension | firmware F6 8.5; the motor pulse runs below 80 mA (17.16) |
specs/cell.md:63:The smallest cell is US 7, 17.6 mAh. Every rule that protects a recording time, and the charge current of 15 mA, use this cell (17.26). The joystick sits lower on US 7 (-84.0) and US 8 (-81.0) than on the other sizes (17.25); the cell ends 0.10 mm past the dam, so it ends earlier there. The motor angles per size (-47.0, -45.5, -44.0, -45.0, -43.5, -42.5, -42.5 degrees for US 7 to 13) do not change the cell. US 5 and US 6 are not built (17.17). Charge time at 15 mA from empty, constant-current part only [CALC, firmware F6 8.1]: 1.17 to 1.57 hours at US 7 and 1.59 to 2.13 hours at US 13.
specs/body.md:5:> **This file gives the outside; the screen pocket, board cavity, band channel and slots are dimensioned in the table below and are not yet cut in the 3D file.** (design/mfg/parts/index.csv status for the body: outer shape only, cavities not cut.)
specs/body.md:8:The one-piece metal body of the ring: a flat block (holds screen, board, glass) that flows through two shoulders into an 8.0 mm wide band that goes round the finger. The band is a hollow channel, open toward the finger, later closed by the band liner (specs/band-liner.md). It carries the pockets and openings for the screen, glass, 2 microphone floor pockets with their holes and seats on the underside, 2 floor slots, the joystick opening and pocket, and the motor pocket. The ring has one control, the joystick (17.11): no record button, no record cap and no opening for one. The four charging pads sit in the liner on the inside of the band, so the body has no pad holes (17.12). Before v5 this page listed microphone wall slots, a record cap opening, 4 pad holes and the joystick at 150 degrees.
specs/body.md:90:Floor slots 2.0 x 0.6 at Y = +-5.9, from X = -3.5 to X = -1.5 (two; model v6.2, architecture 17.27: shortened from 2.6 x 0.6 to keep at least 0.30 mm of metal beside the motor pocket). On ring sizes US 7, 8 and 9 the motor pocket opens into the block's cavity through a window of about 4.0 x 1.1 mm (the pocket grown by 0.30 mm intersected with the cavity grown by 0.30 mm), closed at assembly by the sealant plug SEAL2; on US 10 to 13 a wall of at least 0.30 mm stands there. Motor pocket angle per size: -47.0, -45.5, -44.0, -45.0, -43.5, -42.5, -42.5 degrees on US 7 to 13; the pocket floor lies 0.05 mm under the motor. Earlier text of this line: floor slots 2.6 x 0.6 at X = -2.2 (corrected 2026-11-11 from X = -4.7: inside model, thin-notes.md K7; assembly.md conflict K-A10). The bottom of each slot lies at Z -0.20 on US 7 and 8, then -0.23, -0.63, -1.04, -1.45, -1.85 on US 9 to 13 (17.26, model v6), so each ribbon reaches the band channel at every size. Beside each slot, under the board, 2.6 x 2.2 x 1.03 mm stay free for one ribbon fold (K-A6). Panel pocket = panel + 0.05 per side (27.45 x 12.9), corner radius 0.1. The microphone pockets, holes and seats are in the table above; no microphone slot exists in any wall. These are the modeller's choices; the maker takes the STEP file for the final geometry once the parts helper has delivered it.
specs/body.md:105:Drawing: design/mfg/drawings/body.svg and body.pdf (US 9), and body-us7 to body-us13 (.svg and .pdf) for the other sizes (design/mfg/tools/draw.py). STEP and STL: design/mfg/parts/body/body-us9.step and .stl (also us7, us8, us10, us11, us12, us13), listed in design/mfg/parts/index.csv. Status in that index: outer shape only, cavities not cut; so the pockets, slots and openings of the table above are NOT in the STEP file yet. A reference with cavities: design/mfg/parts/body-cavity-reference/ (reference only, not a made part). Open: the parts helper must deliver the body with the cavities cut.
specs/joystick-nub.md:6:The thumb control: one rounded rectangular nub in a raised frame, on the joystick side of the band at -78 degrees on US 9 to 13 (78 degrees from the top; -81.0 on US 8 and -84.0 on US 7, 17.25; the ring has no record button, the joystick is the one control). It tilts; under it five switches (SW1 up, SW3 down, SW4 toward the hand, SW5 toward the fingertip, Alps SKTAAAE010, 2.6 x 1.6 x 0.53 each) sit in a cross at 2.4 mm from the centre, and the centre switch SW2 sits between them (17.15). A press on the centre closes SW2; a long press of 1.0 s starts or stops a recording (17.11). The nub with its flange is one moulded part (architecture 17.3). Before v5: four switches in a cross at 2.1 mm at +150 degrees.
specs/record-cap.md:1:# Specification: record cap reference CAP2 (design/bom.csv line 78, design/mfg/part-map.csv)
specs/record-cap.md:3:This part does not exist. The ring has one control, the joystick (architecture 17.11). The record cap of earlier versions was removed on 2026-11-10. Do not make this part.
specs/record-cap.md:5:A recording starts and stops by a long press of 1.0 s on the joystick's centre switch SW2 (architecture 17.11; specs/joystick-nub.md).
specs/cover-glass.md:6:The formed cover glass over the whole top face of the block. The face is one arc across the finger, rolled into the side walls and at the two ends. The screen under it stays flat. The glass is bonded to the screen panel by 0.1 mm clear adhesive (ADH1). It is also sealed to the body's glass seat all round by a perimeter seal ring SEAL1, 0.05 thick (Z 2.70 to 2.75), in a seat lowered 0.05 inside the body (model v5; assembly.md step 23, conflict K-A4; architecture 17.23, 17.24; open item S26). The tape is not chosen and its compressed thickness is unknown. Black print everywhere outside the lit area, with one round unprinted window of dia 0.4 at X -12.425, Y -2.95 (hand end, joystick side) for the recording light (17.21). Behind the window a clear plug LP1 (0.4 mm cube) sits on the screen ledge under the adhesive; the glass is 0.959 thick there.
specs/cover-glass.md:41:| recording light window | dia 0.4 mm, at X -12.425, Y -2.95 | ISO 2768-f | round, unprinted, in the black print on the inner face; 2.9 mm outside the lit picture; before 17.21 it was dia 0.6 at X -13.25, Y -4.7 |
specs/cover-glass.md:58:4. Seal and window: the seal tape and its compressed thickness (S26), and whether the recording light is bright enough through the 0.4 mm window (bench test, 17.21; the screen maker confirms the ledge glass is clear there, S25).
